[KANWONDO_PUKTO.FullName]
Gangwondo-pukto

[KANWONDO_PUKTO.Description]
The North Korean province of Gangwondo-pukto is located along the border region. It is mostly highland, but the terrain along the coastline is suitable for vehicle transport.

[HVANHE_PUKTO.FullName]
Hwanghae-pukto

[HVANHE_PUKTO.Description]
The southern border of the Hwanghaebuk-do province follows the 38th parallel. Enemy forces are concentrated to the south. The province is strategically critical to the defense of Pyeongyang, the capital of North Korea.

[KANWONDO_NAMDO.FullName]
Gangwondo-Namdo

[KANWONDO_NAMDO.Description]
The northern border of the Gangwonnam-do province follows the 38th parallel, which divides the Korean Peninsula into North and South Korea. Being a mountainous land, the province does not have any heavy fortifications, and the mountain passes are treacherous: even a small defending force can contain a superior attacking force. The small villages and monasteries have been converted into defensive citadels which are very difficult to capture. Only the rocky paths unsuitable for vehicles remain unoccupied.

[KENGIDO.FullName]
Gyeonggi-do

[KENGIDO.Description]
Being the location of Seoul, the capital of South Korea, Gyeonggi-do is a strategically critical province. While mostly plains, the province is dotted by inhabited localities, many of which have been fortified. Naturally, Seoul itself is protected by South Korea's most experienced troops, and is also surrounded by heavy fortifications. The northern border of the province follows the 38th parallel, which divides Korea into North and South.

[KENSAN_PUKTO.FullName]
Gyeongsangbuk-do

[KENSAN_PUKTO.Description]
Gyeongsangbuk-do provides access to the east coast of the Korean Peninsula. Whoever holds this province can receive supplies by sea, a significant strategic advantage. Gyeongsangbuk-do has numerous mountain valleys, but closer to the east coast, mountains lower, and the valleys widen and flatten. This makes the area very convenient for attacking but difficult to defend.

[CHUNCHON_PUKTO.FullName]
Chungcheongbuk-do

[CHUNCHON_PUKTO.Description]
Chungcheongbuk-do is the main transport corridor to Seoul, the capital of South Korea. Numerous military bases and fortifications are concentrated in this province. Gaining control over Chungcheongbuk-do would provide a significant advantage.

[CHUNCHON_NAMDO.FullName]
Chungcheongnam-do

[CHUNCHON_NAMDO.Description]
A large part of Chungcheongnam-do is occupied by nearly impassable mountains, but there are certain areas which are susceptible to attack, though naturally these are heavily defended and fortified. All of the population centers have been converted into strong points, but some of the rocky paths are impassable to vehicles. This makes them perfect for small infantry squads, which can maneuver covertly and take the enemy by surprise.

[PUSAN.FullName]
Pusan

[PUSAN.Description]
The Pusan Perimeter is the final position of the South Korean troops. This most important strategic area serves as a beachhead for the US forces, and boasts numerous well-defended strong points. Capturing the Pusan Perimeter, as well as Pusan, Ulsan and Pohang ports, would prevent the United Nations Command forces from bringing in reinforcements and supplies.

[KENSAN_NAMDO.FullName]
Gyeongsangnam-do

[KENSAN_NAMDO.Description]
The Gyeongsangnam-do province adjoins the Jeollabuk-do and Gyeongsangbuk-do provinces. It is mostly occupied by mountains, and is home to three national parks: Gayasan, Deogyusan and Jirisan. The highest peak is the Deogyusan mountain (1614 meters). Like the rest of the Korean Peninsula, the province has a monsoon climate: warm and damp summers, and relatively cold and dry winters. The average annual temperature is 10.6 degrees Celsius, and the annual precipitation is 1395.2 mm (as of 1993). Most of the precipitation (850mm) falls during the raining season, which lasts for three summer months.

[CHOPLA_PUKTO.FullName]
Jeollabuk-do

[CHOPLA_PUKTO.Description]
Jeollabuk-do is located on the west coast. Its port is critical to the movement of personnel and supplies. However, with the exception of the coastal areas, the province is occupied by treacherous mountains, so there are no heavy fortifications.

[CHOPLA_NAMDO.FullName]
Jeollanam-do

[CHOPLA_NAMDO.Description]
Jeollanam-do is the southernmost province of South Korea. Mountains dominate its northern region. However, towards the southern and coastal regions, the mountains level out to wide valleys. In terms of strategic significance, the southern part of the province is important to the delivery of supplies by sea, thanks to its numerous large ports. The US military command uses this to its advantage. The capture of Jeollanam-do would mean the interruption of enemy supply lines, as well as access to sea supply routes.

